// expect-ok
// Z3 corpus #13: axis-1 — Reference Monitor pattern via actor encapsulation.
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE PROVES
//
// Trustless delegation is expressible TODAY using existing primitives:
// actors + caps + linear move + non-consuming `.draw()` (step 10).
// No new language constructs needed.
//
// A `FuelMonitor` actor owns a Fuel cap in its state and exposes a
// single narrow operation `Burn(amount)` as a message. Clients hold
// `ActorRef<FuelMonitor>`, never the underlying cap — they cannot
// split, restrict, send, or otherwise manipulate the raw fuel. Their
// only authority is to invoke the monitor's narrow interface, which
// the monitor's handler implementation gates with its own logic
// (`.draw(amount)` — non-consuming, so the monitor's state cap
// persists across many `Burn` calls).
//
// WHY THIS MATTERS
//
// "Caps are first-class linear values, anyone holding them can split
// and restrict" is a design choice that maximizes composability. But
// some policies want STRICTER delegation: "I'll let actor X spend up
// to 50 fuel units, but only via the narrow operations I define." A
// raw Fuel cap given to X would let X split it further, send it to
// other actors, or restrict and re-share — none of which the
// delegator wants. The Reference Monitor pattern enforces the
// narrower interface by NEVER exposing the raw cap: X gets an
// ActorRef<Monitor>, the monitor holds the cap.
//
// This is a meaningful axis-1 expressibility result: a pattern that
// many policy authors will reach for is writable today with
// provable-safety guarantees inherited from the actor + cap system.
// Without `.draw()` (pre-step-10), the Burn handler couldn't be
// invoked more than once, since `.split()` consumes the parent —
// fixture 09 documents that closure.
//
// END-TO-END SHAPE
//
// 1. `Main` holds the full Fuel cap in state.
// 2. `Main.on Start` carves 50 units off via `fuel.draw(50)` — non-
//    consuming, so Main keeps the rest.
// 3. `Main` spawns `FuelMonitor` with the 50-unit sub-cap.
// 4. FuelMonitor's `init(f: Fuel)` stores it as state.
// 5. FuelMonitor's `on Burn(amount)` handler invokes
//    `fuel.draw(amount)` to consume amount units. The sub-cap
//    returned by draw falls out of scope at handler end (Sigil's
//    drop semantics for linear caps).
// 6. Clients with `ActorRef<FuelMonitor>` can `monitor.send(Burn(N))`
//    or `monitor.ask(Burn(N)) -> i64` but cannot access the raw cap.
//
// MUTATION CONTRACT: not applicable. This is an `expect-ok` fixture
// (positive evidence of expressibility). Removing any line would
// break compilation, not flip a verdict.
module sigil;
cap type Fuel {}

actor FuelMonitor {
    state { fuel: Fuel }

    init(f: Fuel) {}

    on Burn(amount: i64) -> i64 {
        let _consumed = fuel.draw(amount);
        return amount;
    }
}

entry actor Main {
    state { fuel: Fuel }

    on Start() -> i64 {
        let monitor_fuel = fuel.draw(50);
        let _monitor = spawn::<FuelMonitor>(monitor_fuel);
        return 1;
    }
}
